.TH "COMPLYCTL" "1" "November 2026" "Complyctl Manual" ""
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
complyctl - compliance assessment CLI using provider-based scanning
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]complyctl\f[R] [command] [flags]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Complyctl is a lightweight compliance runtime that pulls Gemara policies
from an OCI registry and executes scans via providers, producing
compliance reports in multiple formats (EvaluationLog, OSCAL, SARIF,
Markdown).
.SS Providers
.PP
Providers are standalone executables that integrate complyctl with
policy engines such as OpenSCAP, Ampel, and OPA.
Each provider converts policy content into the engine\[cq]s native input
format and translates the raw results back into the schema used by
complyctl for report generation.
.PP
Providers communicate with complyctl via gRPC and can be authored in any
language.
They are distributed separately (e.g., via the
\f[B]complytime-providers\f[R] package) and discovered automatically by
naming convention (\f[B]complyctl-provider-*\f[R]) from the following
directories:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\[ti]/.local/share/complytime/providers/\f[R] (user directory,
takes precedence)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]/usr/libexec/complytime/providers/\f[R] (system directory)
.PP
Run \f[B]complyctl providers\f[R] to list discovered providers on your
system and \f[B]complyctl doctor\f[R] to validate the full stack
including provider health and variable requirements.
.PP
See the complytime-providers repository at
<https://github.com/complytime/complytime-providers> for available
providers and their documentation.
See the provider authoring guide at
<https://github.com/complytime/complytime-providers/blob/main/docs/provider-guide.md>
for writing custom providers.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS init
.PP
Create a workspace configuration file
(\f[B].complytime/complytime.yaml\f[R]).
Interactively prompts for policies and targets.
Errors if the configuration file already exists.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
complyctl init
\f[R]
.fi
.SS get
.PP
Fetch policies and complypacks from OCI registries or a local OCI image
layout into the local cache.
Registry sync performs incremental downloads and uses Docker credential
helpers for authentication.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
complyctl get
complyctl get --skip-verify
complyctl get --from-oci-layout /path/to/oci-layout
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[B]-t\f[R], \f[B]\[en]timeout\f[R] \f[I]duration\f[R]
Maximum time for the get operation (default: \f[B]5m\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\[en]skip-verify\f[R]
Skip signature verification for fetched artifacts.
.TP
\f[B]\[en]from-oci-layout\f[R] \f[I]path\f[R]
Import digest-pinned configured policies and complypacks from a local
OCI image layout.
.SS Offline provisioning
.PP
On a disconnected system, configure policies and complypacks with
digest-pinned URLs, then import a local OCI image layout:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
complyctl get --from-oci-layout /path/to/oci-layout
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The configured digest selects each artifact from the layout without
contacting the registry.
If verification is configured, add \f[C]--skip-verify\f[R]; offline
signature verification is not yet supported and imported entries remain
unverified.
.SS list
.PP
List cached Gemara policies with their evaluator, control count, digest,
and verification status.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
complyctl list
complyctl list --policy-id nist-800-53-r5
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[B]\[en]policy-id\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Filter output to a single policy.
.SS generate
.PP
Resolve the policy dependency graph from cache, extract assessment
configurations, apply parameter overrides from
\f[B]complytime.yaml\f[R], and dispatch to the matching provider via the
Generate RPC.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
complyctl generate --policy-id nist-800-53-r5
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[B]-p\f[R], \f[B]\[en]policy-id\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Policy ID to generate (\f[B]required\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]-t\f[R], \f[B]\[en]timeout\f[R] \f[I]duration\f[R]
Maximum time for the generate operation (default: \f[B]5m\f[R]).
.SS scan
.PP
Scan targets and produce compliance reports.
When a target is specified and references exactly one policy,
\f[B]\[en]policy-id\f[R] is inferred.
At least one of \f[I][target]\f[R] or \f[B]\[en]policy-id\f[R] is
required.
.PP
Output is written to \f[B].complytime/scan/\f[R].
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
complyctl scan --policy-id my-policy
complyctl scan my-target
complyctl scan my-target --policy-id my-policy --format pretty
complyctl scan --policy-id my-policy --format oscal,sarif
complyctl scan --policy-id my-policy --format all
complyctl scan --policy-id my-policy --log-format json
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[B]-p\f[R], \f[B]\[en]policy-id\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Policy ID to scan.
.TP
\f[B]-f\f[R], \f[B]\[en]format\f[R] \f[I]strings\f[R]
Additional output format(s): \f[B]oscal\f[R], \f[B]pretty\f[R]
(Markdown), \f[B]sarif\f[R], \f[B]all\f[R] (all three).
Accepts comma-separated values (\f[C]--format oscal,sarif\f[R]) or
repeated flags (\f[C]--format oscal --format sarif\f[R]).
The default EvaluationLog is always produced regardless of this flag.
.TP
\f[B]-t\f[R], \f[B]\[en]timeout\f[R] \f[I]duration\f[R]
Maximum time for the scan operation (default: \f[B]5m\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\[en]show-passing\f[R]
Include passing controls in the terminal scan summary table (default:
\f[B]true\f[R]).
Can also be set via \f[B]COMPLYTIME_SHOW_PASSING\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\[en]log-format\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
EvaluationLog serialization format: \f[B]yaml\f[R], \f[B]json\f[R]
(default: \f[B]yaml\f[R]).
Can also be set via \f[B]COMPLYTIME_LOG_FORMAT\f[R].
.SS clean
.PP
Expire managed report/evaluation groups beneath \f[B].complytime/\f[R]
in the selected workspace.
Successful expiry permanently removes both the reports and their
supporting evaluation directories, including all contained outputs.
Save anything needed for audit elsewhere and preview before deleting:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
complyctl clean --older-than 30d --dry-run
complyctl clean --older-than 30d
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[B]\[en]older-than\f[R] \f[I]days\f[R]d
Required positive whole days, each exactly 24 hours; no default.
Accepts \f[B]1d\f[R] through \f[B]106751d\f[R], without leading zeroes.
Rejects zero, negative, fractional, whitespace-padded, unsupported-unit,
and overflowing values.
.TP
\f[B]\[en]dry-run\f[R]
Preview complete groups without changing workspace files (default:
\f[B]false\f[R]).
Does not create configuration, lock files, activity records, reports,
evidence, or debug logs.
Preview releases access when done; later cleanup independently repeats
discovery and preflight.
.PP
No positional arguments are accepted.
Workspace precedence is \f[B]\[en]workspace\f[R] (or \f[B]-w\f[R]), then
\f[B]COMPLYTIME_WORKSPACE\f[R], then the current directory.
Select the existing project root containing \f[B].complytime/\f[R].
Cleanup does not initialize a missing workspace or require
configuration, registry credentials, policy resolution, or provider
installation.
Results use stdout; diagnostics use stderr even with
\f[B]\[en]debug\f[R], without creating or modifying the workspace log
file.
.SS Scope and expiry
.PP
Cleanup recognizes retained-layout evaluation directories beneath only
\f[B].complytime/ampel/results/\f[R],
\f[B].complytime/opa/results/\f[R], and
\f[B].complytime/openscap/results/\f[R].
Valid intermediate labels contain 1\[en]80 ASCII letters, digits,
underscores, or hyphens.
Evaluation names use \f[B]evaluation-\f[R], a UTC collection-start
timestamp in \f[B]20060102T150405Z\f[R] format, a hyphen, and exactly 32
lowercase hexadecimal characters.
Flat legacy files and unrecognized layouts remain untouched.
.PP
Managed reports live directly under \f[B].complytime/scan/\f[R]:
YAML/JSON EvaluationLogs, Markdown with an embedded EvaluationLog,
OSCAL, and SARIF variants.
Validated content establishes relationships; filenames alone never prove
ownership.
Reports sharing any evaluation join one group transitively, even when
they cite different files within it.
An unreferenced or incomplete evaluation can qualify only after a
complete managed-report inventory establishes no report references it.
.PP
Every evaluation in a group must start strictly before one captured UTC
cutoff.
Age uses collection start, not modification times, report filenames, or
optional metadata.
A newer, exact-cutoff, future, or invalid start retains the whole group.
Reports follow their connected evaluations\[cq] ages.
Reports without an establishable evaluation age stay.
.PP
Cleanup preserves CLI logs, configuration, scan inputs, working clones,
generation state, caches, unrelated files, and other workspaces.
It does not prune labels or managed roots, fetch remote evidence, or
expand its scope through evidence references.
External or renamed report copies are outside managed scope; cleanup
cannot guarantee their references remain valid.
Renaming is not an override: unknown report-like files inside the report
root can block cleanup.
.PP
Current SARIF output lacks originating-log and evidence provenance.
Cleanup therefore refuses workspace planning when a managed SARIF report
is present, including an empty all-passing report.
It never guesses associations from a matching filename or silently skips
SARIF.
Unbounded report ambiguity likewise refuses the workspace before
deletion.
.SS Safety, support, and failures
.PP
Whole-group preflight retains every member unchanged if any member has a
detectable safety or access failure.
Confined traversal refuses symlinks, special files, replaced candidates,
and mount crossings.
Eligible groups lose all evidence before report removal begins.
Unexpected removal failures stop the affected group, retain what
remains, print exact progress, and return nonzero; independent safe
groups can continue.
An evidence failure leaves all group reports, possibly referencing
already removed evidence.
A report failure can leave some reports after all evidence is gone.
There is no rollback.
.PP
Cleanup and preview require exclusive workspace access.
Updated hosts coordinate scan/generation, relevant \f[B]get\f[R]
artifact removal, and \f[B]init\f[R]; conflicting access fails promptly.
Scan protection extends through report publication and normal provider
teardown.
Normal RPC completion and teardown allow removal of the invocation\[cq]s
own activity record, including handled application errors.
Host interruption, forced or abnormal termination, transport failure, or
uncertain cancellation leaves unresolved activity that blocks cleanup
even after lock release.
Normal completion is not proof that arbitrary descendant tools exited.
.PP
Cleanup targets Linux and macOS on amd64 and arm64.
Allowed local filesystems are ext4, XFS, Btrfs, and tmpfs on Linux, and
APFS on macOS.
Other filesystems, including network, FUSE, overlay, and HFS+, are
refused.
OS support and filesystem support are separate; unavailable
locking/confinement or unsafe mount layouts also cause refusal.
These targets do not imply completed native runtime verification on
every platform.
Windows is outside the cleanup support boundary.
.PP
Stop older hosts, direct provider callers, and independent writers
before cleanup: host locks do not stop them.
Keep directory and mount layouts stable.
Unchanged provider binaries work under updated hosts, but provider
availability does not limit cleanup of existing evidence.
.PP
Unresolved activity errors identify exact record paths under
\f[B].complytime/coordination/operations/\f[R].
Pause new scans and stop all writers, including provider-launched tools.
If shutdown is uncertain, restart the complete writer machine/VM or stop
its complete isolated container environment, prevent job restarts, and
stop writers in other environments too.
Restarting a terminal or complyctl alone is insufficient.
Only then manually remove the exact identified records, not the
coordination directory or unrelated files.
Records and PIDs are diagnostic hints, not authority to kill processes.
There is no force or automatic stale-record override.
Rerun preview; record removal never bypasses a live host lock.
See the evidence guide\[cq]s Interrupted-Scan
Recovery (https://github.com/complytime/complyctl/blob/main/docs/EVIDENCE.md#interrupted-scan-recovery)
for the full procedure.
.PP
Output includes workspace, UTC cutoff, consequences, stable group
membership, blockers, reasons, exclusions, and totals.
Preview uses \f[B]would-delete\f[R] or \f[B]retained\f[R]; execution
uses \f[B]deleted\f[R], \f[B]partially-deleted\f[R], or
\f[B]retained\f[R] and prints successful removals.
No matches, normal age exclusions, and repeated successful cleanup exit
zero.
Invalid arguments, missing workspaces, coordination/inventory refusal,
safety/access failures, removal errors, and output errors exit nonzero,
even when independent groups succeeded.
.SS doctor
.PP
Run pre-flight diagnostics on the workspace.
Checks provider discovery, policy cache integrity, configuration
validation, complypack availability, and cache health (disk usage,
orphaned versions).
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
complyctl doctor
complyctl doctor --verbose
complyctl doctor --format text
complyctl doctor --format json
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[B]\[en]verbose\f[R]
Expand per-provider variable detail.
.TP
\f[B]-f\f[R], \f[B]\[en]format\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Output format: \f[B]human\f[R] (emoji, default), \f[B]text\f[R]
(grep-friendly plain labels: [PASS]/[FAIL]/[WARN]), \f[B]json\f[R]
(structured machine output).
When \f[B]NO_COLOR\f[R] is set, \f[B]text\f[R] is selected
automatically.
.SS providers
.PP
List discovered scanning providers with their evaluator ID, path, health
status, version, and cached complypack versions.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
complyctl providers
\f[R]
.fi
.SS version
.PP
Print the version.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
complyctl version
\f[R]
.fi
.SS completion
.PP
Generate shell completion scripts for Bash, Zsh, Fish, or PowerShell.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
complyctl completion bash
complyctl completion zsh
complyctl completion fish
\f[R]
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
These options apply to all commands.
.TP
\f[B]-d\f[R], \f[B]\[en]debug\f[R]
Output debug logs to stderr and the workspace log file.
Exception: \f[B]clean\f[R], including preview, uses stderr only and
leaves logs unchanged.
.TP
\f[B]-w\f[R], \f[B]\[en]workspace\f[R] \f[I]directory\f[R]
Workspace directory.
Takes precedence over \f[B]COMPLYTIME_WORKSPACE\f[R]; otherwise defaults
to the current directory.
.TP
\f[B]-h\f[R], \f[B]\[en]help\f[R]
Show help for complyctl or any subcommand.
.PP
Run \f[B]complyctl [command] \[en]help\f[R] for more information about a
specific command.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
Complyctl reads its workspace configuration from
\f[B].complytime/complytime.yaml\f[R] in the current (or
\f[B]\[en]workspace\f[R]) directory.
Create the file with \f[B]complyctl init\f[R] or write it manually.
.SS policies
.PP
OCI references to Gemara policy bundles.
At least one is required.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
policies:
  - url: ghcr.io/myorg/policies/nist-800-53-r5:v1.0.0
    id: nist          # optional; derived from URL path if omitted
  - url: ghcr.io/myorg/policies/cis-benchmark:latest
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[B]url\f[R] (required)
Full OCI reference including registry, repository, and optional
\f[B]:tag\f[R] or \f[B]\[at]sha256:digest\f[R] (e.g.,
\f[B]registry.example.com/policies/my-policy:v1.0\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]id\f[R] (optional)
Short alias used by \f[B]targets[].policies\f[R] to reference this
entry.
If omitted, the last path segment of the URL is used (e.g.,
\f[B]ghcr.io/org/policies/nist-r5:v1.0\f[R] derives \f[B]nist-r5\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]verification\f[R] (optional)
Per-entry verification override.
Same fields as the workspace-level \f[B]verification\f[R] block (see
below).
Overrides the workspace-level config for this entry.
.TP
\f[B]skip_verify\f[R] (optional)
Set to \f[B]true\f[R] to skip verification for this entry even when
workspace-level verification is configured.
Mutually exclusive with per-entry \f[B]verification\f[R].
.SS complypacks
.PP
OCI references to provider-specific content bundles (Rego policies,
XCCDF profiles, mapping files).
Fetched alongside policies during \f[B]complyctl get\f[R].
Each complypack is matched to a provider by its evaluator-id.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
complypacks:
  - url: ghcr.io/myorg/complypacks/ampel-bp:v1.0.0
    id: ampel-bp-pack
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Fields are the same as \f[B]policies\f[R] (url, id, verification,
skip_verify).
Run \f[B]complyctl providers\f[R] to see which providers have
complypacks and their evaluator IDs.
.SS targets
.PP
Systems to evaluate.
Each target selects one or more policies by their effective ID and
provides provider-specific variables.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
targets:
  - id: my-repo
    policies:
      - nist
    variables:
      url: https://github.com/myorg/myrepo
      access_token: ${GITHUB_TOKEN}
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[B]id\f[R] (required)
Scan target identifier.
Must be unique across all targets.
.TP
\f[B]policies\f[R] (required)
List of effective policy IDs to evaluate against this target.
Each must match a \f[B]policies[].id\f[R] or the auto-derived ID from
\f[B]policies[].url\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]variables\f[R] (optional)
Provider-specific key-value pairs passed to the provider during scan.
Supports \f[B]${VAR}\f[R] environment variable substitution.
Unset variables cause a configuration error.
.PP
Run \f[B]complyctl doctor \[en]verbose\f[R] to see the required and
optional variables for each target, including one-of groups where at
least one variable in the group must be provided (e.g., \f[B]url\f[R] or
\f[B]input_path\f[R]).
.SS variables
.PP
Workspace-scoped constants passed to all providers.
Unlike target variables, workspace variables do \f[B]not\f[R] support
\f[B]${VAR}\f[R] substitution \[em] they are passed as-is.
Place environment-dependent values in \f[B]targets[].variables\f[R]
instead.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
variables:
  output_dir: /tmp/scan-results
\f[R]
.fi
.SS verification
.PP
OCI artifact signature verification.
Two mutually exclusive modes are supported: keyless (Sigstore OIDC) and
keyed (cosign key-pair).
When omitted, verification is skipped.
.PP
Keyless verification (GitHub Actions example):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
verification:
  issuer: https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com
  identity: https://github.com/myorg/myrepo/.github/workflows/release.yml\[at]refs/tags/*
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Keyed verification (public key):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
verification:
  key: /path/to/cosign.pub
\f[R]
.fi
.TP
\f[B]issuer\f[R] (keyless)
OIDC token issuer URL (e.g.,
\f[B]https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com\f[R]).
Requires \f[B]identity\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]identity\f[R] (keyless)
Expected SAN identity in the signing certificate.
Supports glob patterns (e.g., \f[B]*\[at]refs/tags/*\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]key\f[R] (keyed)
Path to a PEM-encoded public key.
Mutually exclusive with \f[B]issuer\f[R]/\f[B]identity\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]trusted_root\f[R] (keyless, optional)
Path to a \f[B]trusted_root.json\f[R] file for keyless verification
against private Sigstore instances.
Mutually exclusive with \f[B]key\f[R]; requires \f[B]issuer\f[R] and
\f[B]identity\f[R].
.PP
Per-entry verification can be set on individual \f[B]policies[]\f[R] or
\f[B]complypacks[]\f[R] entries, overriding the workspace-level config:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
policies:
  - url: ghcr.io/myorg/policies/internal-policy:v1.0
    verification:
      key: /path/to/internal.pub
  - url: ghcr.io/thirdparty/policy:v2.0
    skip_verify: true
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Complete example
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
policies:
  - url: quay.io/complytime/policies-ampel-branch-protection:latest
    id: ampel-bp
complypacks:
  - url: ghcr.io/myorg/complypacks/ampel-bp:v1.0.0
    id: ampel-bp-pack
variables:
  output_dir: /tmp/results
verification:
  issuer: https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com
  identity: https://github.com/complytime/complyctl/.github/workflows/release.yml\[at]refs/tags/*
targets:
  - id: my-repo
    policies:
      - ampel-bp
    variables:
      url: https://github.com/myorg/myrepo
      access_token: ${GITHUB_TOKEN}
\f[R]
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]COMPLYTIME_WORKSPACE\f[R]
Override the workspace directory used by complyctl.
When set, complyctl resolves configuration and output paths relative to
this directory.
The \f[B]\[en]workspace\f[R] flag takes precedence.
.TP
\f[B]COMPLYTIME_SHOW_PASSING\f[R]
When set to \f[B]false\f[R], exclude passing controls from the terminal
scan summary table.
Default: \f[B]true\f[R] (show all controls).
The \f[B]\[en]show-passing\f[R] flag takes precedence.
.TP
\f[B]COMPLYTIME_LOG_FORMAT\f[R]
EvaluationLog serialization format: \f[B]yaml\f[R] or \f[B]json\f[R].
Default: \f[B]yaml\f[R].
The \f[B]\[en]log-format\f[R] flag takes precedence.
.TP
\f[B]COMPLYTIME_CACHE_VERSIONS\f[R]
Number of complypack versions to retain per evaluator-id in the local
cache (\[ti]/.cache/complytime/complypacks/).
Default: \f[B]1\f[R].
Values less than 1 are clamped to 1.
.TP
\f[B]COMPLYTIME_SYSTEM_PROVIDER_DIR\f[R]
Override the system-wide provider directory that is scanned after the
user provider directory.
Providers in the user directory take precedence.
Must be an absolute path; a relative path is ignored.
Default: \f[B]/usr/libexec/complytime/providers\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]COMPLYTIME_MACHINE_ID_FILE\f[R]
Override the path to the machine-id file used by providers when
constructing evidence host remarks.
Default: \f[B]/etc/machine-id\f[R].
The file must contain a non-empty hexadecimal string no larger than 1
KB.
If unreadable or invalid, the machine-id is silently omitted from the
remarks.
.TP
\f[B]NO_COLOR\f[R]
When set (any non-empty value), \f[B]complyctl doctor\f[R] automatically
selects \f[B]text\f[R] format instead of the default human-readable
emoji output.
Follows the NO_COLOR convention at https://no-color.org.
Has no effect when \f[B]\[en]format\f[R] is specified explicitly.
.SH EXIT CODES
.TP
\f[B]0\f[R]
Command completed successfully.
Scan findings (passed, failed, not applicable) are reported in the
output but do not affect the exit code.
Policy findings are data, not errors.
.TP
\f[B]1\f[R]
An operational error occurred.
This includes provider failures, invalid configuration, or zero
requirements assessed.
Reports and summaries are written before the non-zero exit so partial
results remain available.
.PP
For \f[B]clean\f[R], normal age exclusions, no matches, and repeated
successful cleanup return \f[B]0\f[R].
Validation, coordination, discovery, preflight, removal, or output
errors return \f[B]1\f[R].
Returned cleanup results are printed even on failure; workspace-wide
refusal can provide no plan.
.PP
To gate a pipeline on compliance results, parse the scan output (SARIF,
OSCAL) with your policy engine rather than relying on the exit code.
.SH OUTPUT
.TP
\f[B]Scan reports\f[R]
The \f[B]scan\f[R] command writes reports to \f[B].complytime/scan/\f[R]
in each enabled format.
Report filenames follow the pattern:
.RS
.PP
\f[I]prefix\f[R]-\f[I]policy-id\f[R]-\f[I]target-id\f[R]-\f[I]YYYYMMDD\f[R]-\f[I]HHMMSS\f[R].\f[I]ext\f[R]
.RE
.PP
When no target ID is present (single-target scans), the target segment
is omitted.
Path separators in policy and target IDs are replaced with dashes.
.TP
\f[B]Evidence source provenance\f[R]
Each evidence entry in the EvaluationLog may include a \f[B]source\f[R]
block identifying where the data was collected from.
The source block contains five fields:
.TP
\f[I]reference-id\f[R]
Names the external system or Gemara artifact the evidence was collected
from.
Ties the evidence to a \f[B]mapping-references\f[R] entry in the
EvaluationLog metadata.
.TP
\f[I]coordinate\f[R]
Precise location within the referenced system (API path, file path, JSON
path).
Mutually exclusive with \f[B]entry-id\f[R].
.TP
\f[I]entry-id\f[R]
Specific entry within a referenced Gemara artifact.
Mutually exclusive with \f[B]coordinate\f[R].
.TP
\f[I]digest\f[R]
Cryptographic hash of the observed content at collection time.
Format: \f[I]algorithm\f[R]:\f[I]encoded\f[R] (e.g.,
sha256:abc123\&...).
.TP
\f[I]remarks\f[R]
Provider prose about collection context, host identification, or
caveats.
For providers that support it, the standard format is \f[B]Collected on
host \[lq]hostname\[rq] (machine-id: hexstring)\f[R], where the
machine-id is read from \f[B]/etc/machine-id\f[R] (overridable via
\f[B]COMPLYTIME_MACHINE_ID_FILE\f[R]).
.PP
In the Markdown report (\f[B]\[en]format pretty\f[R]), evidence source
is rendered as \f[B]source:\f[R] \f[I]reference-id\f[R] **\[at]**
\f[I]coordinate\f[R].
The full set of five fields is available in EvaluationLog YAML/JSON
output.
Evidence source requires provider support; when absent, the source block
is omitted.
.PP
For a detailed walkthrough and field reference, see the Evidence Source
Provenance documentation at
<https://github.com/complytime/complyctl/blob/main/docs/EVIDENCE.md>.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[B]\[ti]/.cache/complytime/policies/\f[R]
OCI Layout cache for fetched policies (one store per policy ID).
Follows the XDG Base Directory Specification
(\f[B]$XDG_CACHE_HOME\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\[ti]/.cache/complytime/complypacks/\f[R]
Complypack cache with version retention per evaluator-id.
.TP
\f[B]\[ti]/.local/share/complytime/state.json\f[R]
Digest tracking for incremental policy sync.
Follows the XDG Base Directory Specification (\f[B]$XDG_DATA_HOME\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\[ti]/.local/share/complytime/providers/\f[R]
User-installed provider executables (takes precedence over system
directory).
.TP
\f[B]/usr/libexec/complytime/providers/\f[R]
System-installed provider executables.
.TP
\f[B].complytime/complytime.yaml\f[R]
Workspace configuration file.
See the \f[B]CONFIGURATION\f[R] section for the full schema reference.
.TP
\f[B].complytime/scan/\f[R]
Scan output reports (EvaluationLog, OSCAL, SARIF, Markdown).
Filenames follow the pattern
\f[I]prefix\f[R]-\f[I]policy-id\f[R]-\f[I]target-id\f[R]-\f[I]YYYYMMDD\f[R]-\f[I]HHMMSS\f[R].\f[I]ext\f[R]
(e.g., \f[B]evaluation-log-pol-v1-web-20260729-143012.yaml\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B].complytime/complyctl.log\f[R]
Debug log file (created when \f[B]\[en]debug\f[R] is used, except by
\f[B]clean\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B].complytime/{ampel,opa,openscap}/results/\f[R]
Provider results roots.
Recognized evaluation directories and all their contents are indivisible
evidence members for \f[B]clean\f[R].
.TP
\f[B].complytime/coordination/operations/\f[R]
Host activity records persisted before provider startup.
Unresolved records block cleanup and require the stopped-writer recovery
procedure above; they are not age-based cleanup candidates.
